---
// Gocar — Overview. The section's landing: what the tool is, how it relates to
// the fork map (the map is its exported dataset), how its claims were checked,
// and what it deliberately does not do.
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import GocarNav from "../../components/GocarNav.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import { GOCAR_CRATE, mapDoc } from "../../data/gocar";
---

<Layout
    title="Gocar — gpui-archipelago"
    description="Gocar is a Cargo subcommand for binding GPUI forks: it records which fork and release a project binds, resolves it, and verifies the result. The fork map is a view of what it measures."
>
    <GocarNav />

    <PageHeader title="Gocar" meta={GOCAR_CRATE.description} />

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2
                class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight"
            >
                What it is
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    Gocar is a <code>cargo</code> subcommand. You tell it which
                    fork and release your project binds, and from then on that
                    binding is recorded, resolved, and verified by the tool
                    rather than by a <code>package = "…"</code> rename someone
                    has to get right by hand:
                </p>
            </div>
            <pre class="command-block"><code>{`${GOCAR_CRATE.install}\ncargo gocar new my-app --provider gpui-unofficial`}</code></pre>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    That matters because the forks publish the same project
                    under six package names, with unsynchronised versions. A
                    hand-edited binding that is subtly wrong usually surfaces as
                    a compile error deep in a build — the worst place to find
                    out.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2
                class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight"
            >
                The fork map is its dataset
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    <code>cargo gocar export-fork-map</code> writes the bundle
                    the <a href="/map/">Fork Map</a> renders
                    (<code>gocar.forkmap.manifest.v1</code>). The map is a
                    read-only view of what Gocar measured: it compares the
                    measured surface across forks and releases, and it never
                    fills a gap with a guess.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2
                class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight"
            >
                How the claims are checked
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    The studies behind the map's badges ran on real crates.io
                    artifacts — real compiles, real <code>rustc</code> versions,
                    real kits — not on fixtures. They are readable in the map
                    itself, from <a href="/map/#/docs">its documents index</a>,
                    and every compile claim the map makes links the study it
                    came from.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section border-b border-border-divider last:border-b-0">
        <div class="content">
            <h2
                class="mb-8 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-tight"
            >
                What it does not do
            </h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    V1 does not rewrite your code. Where a change is needed it
                    names the site and the reason, and leaves the edit to you:
                    the study that ran the whole flow on a real app found the
                    auto-driven share of a real break is zero, and left the move
                    manual on purpose — <a href={mapDoc("9")}>doc 09</a>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    It also refuses what it cannot vouch for: a prerelease floor
                    (<a href={mapDoc("7")}>doc 07</a>), and a second fork
                    anywhere in the dependency graph (<a href={mapDoc("10")}
                        >doc 10</a
                    >).
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>
